%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 34a6bb67aa8c2e94392484b7355c5a0d, type: 3}
  m_Name: BrainDatabase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  brainAreas:
  - brainArea: 0
    shortDescription: Used for fine motor skills.
    longDescription: "Your cerebellum maintains your balance, posture, coordination
      and fine motor skills. It\u2019s a small, half-circle shape that\u2019s located
      in the back of your brain around your brainstem."
  - brainArea: 1
    shortDescription: Deals with concious actions.
    longDescription: "Your cerebrum interprets your five senses. It regulates conscious
      actions that require thinking, like your speech, memory, behavior, personality,
      movement, reasoning and judgment. It\u2019s the largest part of your brain,
      divided into two halves: the left and right hemispheres. The two halves connect
      by nerve fiber bundles (white matter) called the corpus callosum."
  - brainArea: 2
    shortDescription: Connects the right and left hemispheres (sides).
    longDescription: The corpus callosum is a structure in the middle of your brain
      that connects the right and left hemispheres (sides). The structure is made
      up of nerve fibers. Nerve fibers help the left and right sides of your brain
      talk to each other by sending signals. This communication pathway allows you
      to coordinate many essential functions that are part of your daily routine.
  - brainArea: 3
    shortDescription: Connects the right and left hemispheres (sides).
    longDescription: The corpus callosum is a structure in the middle of your brain
      that connects the right and left hemispheres (sides). The structure is made
      up of nerve fibers. Nerve fibers help the left and right sides of your brain
      talk to each other by sending signals. This communication pathway allows you
      to coordinate many essential functions that are part of your daily routine.
  - brainArea: 4
    shortDescription: Deals with fear.
    longDescription: "Your amygdala is a small, almond-shaped structure inside of
      your brain. It\u2019s part of a larger network in your brain called the limbic
      system. When it comes to your survival, your amygdala and limbic system are
      extremely important. These are parts of your brain that automatically detect
      danger. They also play a role in behavior, emotional control and learning."
  - brainArea: 5
    shortDescription: Deals with fear.
    longDescription: "Your amygdala is a small, almond-shaped structure inside of
      your brain. It\u2019s part of a larger network in your brain called the limbic
      system. When it comes to your survival, your amygdala and limbic system are
      extremely important. These are parts of your brain that automatically detect
      danger. They also play a role in behavior, emotional control and learning."
  - brainArea: 6
    shortDescription: Connects the hemispheres.
    longDescription: The anterior commissure is a white matter tract in the brain
      that connects the two cerebral hemispheres, linking various structures including
      the temporal lobes and olfactory pathways.
  - brainArea: 7
    shortDescription: "It\u2019s a part of your endocrine system and controls the
      function of several other endocrine glands."
    longDescription: "The anterior pituitary, also called adenohypophysis, is the
      front lobe of your pituitary gland. This is a small, pea-sized gland located
      at the base of your brain, below your hypothalamus. It\u2019s a part of your
      endocrine system and controls the function of several other endocrine glands. 
      Your pituitary gland consists of two lobes: the anterior (front) lobe and the
      posterior (back) lobe. The anterior pituitary creates and releases six different
      hormones, which regulate various bodily processes, including:  Growth, Metabolism,
      Reproduction, Stress response, Lactation"
  - brainArea: 8
    shortDescription: Deals with voluntary movements.
    longDescription: "The basal ganglia are best known for how they help your brain
      control your body\u2019s movements. However, ongoing research continues to
      uncover other ways that the basal ganglia interact with other parts of your
      brain. Though experts continue to uncover more about the inner workings of
      the basal ganglia, there\u2019s much about them that remains unknown."
  - brainArea: 9
    shortDescription: Connects the hippocampus to other parts of the brain.
    longDescription: The fornix of the brain is a C-shaped bundle of nerve fibers,
      part of the limbic system, that serves as the main output tract of the hippocampus.
      It is crucial for memory, especially long-term and episodic memory recall,
      and connects the hippocampus to other brain regions involved in emotion and
      cognition.
  - brainArea: 10
    shortDescription: Deals with memory.
    longDescription: 'Your hippocampus is responsible for the following cognitive
      functions:  Learning, Short- and long-term memory, Visual-spatial memory (remembering
      the position of your body in relation to nearby objects), Verbal memory (remembering
      the right words to say), Declarative (or explicit) memory (the recollection
      of facts or experiences).'
  - brainArea: 11
    shortDescription: "The hypothalamus is a structure deep within your brain. It\u2019s
      the main link between your endocrine system and your nervous system. Your hypothalamus
      keeps your body balanced in a stable state called homeostasis."
    longDescription: "Your hypothalamus receives chemical messages from nerve cells
      in your brain and from nerve cells in your body (your peripheral nervous system),
      which also respond to signals outside your body.  Your hypothalamus\u2019s
      main function is to react to these messages to keep your body in a stable state
      or internal balance."
  - brainArea: 12
    shortDescription: It plays a crucial role in regulating fluid and electrolyte
      balance.
    longDescription: 'The lamina terminalis is a thin membrane forming the anterior
      wall of the third ventricle, located between the optic chiasm and the anterior
      commissure. It plays a crucial role in regulating fluid and electrolyte balance
      by sensing blood osmolarity through circumventricular organs like the organum
      vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT) and the subfornical organ (SFO),
      which influence thirst and water content in the body. Embryologically, it is
      the closed anterior neuropore.  '
  - brainArea: 13
    shortDescription: Plays a crucial role in memory.
    longDescription: The mammillary bodies are a pair of nuclei located on the posterior
      part of the hypothalamus that are crucial for memory, particularly spatial
      and episodic memory
  - brainArea: 14
    shortDescription: Deals with smell.
    longDescription: The olfactory bulb is a structure in the forebrain that processes
      smell information. It receives input from olfactory receptors in the nose,
      which are nerve cells that detect odors. The bulb then relays this processed
      information to other brain regions like the amygdala and hippocampus, contributing
      to emotions and memories associated with smell
  - brainArea: 15
    shortDescription: Deals with smell.
    longDescription: The olfactory bulb is a structure in the forebrain that processes
      smell information. It receives input from olfactory receptors in the nose,
      which are nerve cells that detect odors. The bulb then relays this processed
      information to other brain regions like the amygdala and hippocampus, contributing
      to emotions and memories associated with smell
  - brainArea: 16
    shortDescription: Deals with hormones.
    longDescription: 'The posterior pituitary is the back lobe of the pituitary gland
      that stores and releases two hormones, antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and oxytocin,
      which are produced in the hypothalamus. ADH helps regulate water retention
      and blood pressure by acting on the kidneys, while oxytocin is involved in
      uterine contractions during childbirth and the milk ejection reflex during
      lactation.  '
  - brainArea: 17
    shortDescription: Relays sensory information to brain.
    longDescription: "Your thalamus is an egg-shaped structure in the middle of your
      brain. It\u2019s known as a relay station of all incoming motor (movement)
      and sensory information \u2014 hearing, taste, sight and touch (but not smell)
      \u2014 from your body to your brain. Like a relay or train station, all information
      must first pass through your thalamus before being routed or directed to its
      destination in your brain\u2019s cerebral cortex (the outermost layer of your
      brain) for further processing and interpretation."
  - brainArea: 18
    shortDescription: Relays sensory information to brain.
    longDescription: "Your thalamus is an egg-shaped structure in the middle of your
      brain. It\u2019s known as a relay station of all incoming motor (movement)
      and sensory information \u2014 hearing, taste, sight and touch (but not smell)
      \u2014 from your body to your brain. Like a relay or train station, all information
      must first pass through your thalamus before being routed or directed to its
      destination in your brain\u2019s cerebral cortex (the outermost layer of your
      brain) for further processing and interpretation."
  - brainArea: 19
    shortDescription: Part of the endocrine system.
    longDescription: "Your thalamus is an egg-shaped structure in the middle of your
      brain. It\u2019s known as a relay station of all incoming motor (movement)
      and sensory information \u2014 hearing, taste, sight and touch (but not smell)
      \u2014 from your body to your brain. Like a relay or train station, all information
      must first pass through your thalamus before being routed or directed to its
      destination in your brain\u2019s cerebral cortex (the outermost layer of your
      brain) for further processing and interpretation."
  - brainArea: 20
    shortDescription: Protects brain wtih CSF.
    longDescription: The ventricles are a series of four fluid-filled cavities in
      the brain that produce, contain, and circulate cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). They
      are the two lateral ventricles, the third ventricle, and the fourth ventricle.
      CSF protects the brain from injury, provides nutrients, removes waste, and
      cushions the brain and spinal cord.
  - brainArea: 21
    shortDescription: Connects pons and medulla.
    longDescription: The midbrain is the smallest part of the brainstem, located
      between the forebrain and the hindbrain. It serves as a crucial relay center
      for sensory and motor pathways and is responsible for functions like eye movement,
      visual and auditory processing, and motor control. Key structures include the
      tectum (with the superior and inferior colliculi), the tegmentum, the cerebral
      aqueduct, and the cerebral peduncles
  - brainArea: 22
    shortDescription: It forms a key connection between your brain above it and your
      medulla oblongata and spinal cord below it.
    longDescription: Your pons is the second-lowest section of your brainstem, just
      above your medulla oblongata. It forms a key connection between your brain
      above it and your medulla oblongata and spinal cord below it.  Your pons is
      a key merging point for several of your cranial nerves, which are nerves with
      direct connections to your brain. Those nerve connections are vital, helping
      with several of the senses on or in your head, plus your ability to move various
      parts of your face and mouth.
  - brainArea: 23
    shortDescription: Runs through your vertebrae.
    longDescription: "Your spinal cord is a cylinder-shaped tube of tissue that runs
      through the center of your spine, from your brainstem to your lower back. It\u2019s
      made of nerves and cells that carry messages from your brain to the rest of
      your body. Your spinal cord is one of the main parts of your nervous system."
